; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; identity_drivers.ms1 - patterns that mark @self-beliefs as identity-bearing.
;
; Loaded by Merlin at ontology-load time (identity_drivers_load, called from
; t_simulator::load_ontology). The runtime queries is_identity_driver and
; identity_driver_weight test a committed belief against these rows; only
; {@self <label> [<target>]} beliefs match (foreign subjects can't drive
; @self's identity).
;
; A matched belief contributes :weight to Phase 9's identity-anchor
; accumulator. Once enough corroborating identity-driver beliefs have piled
; up about the same theme, Phase 9 promotes the belief to is_identity_anchor
; (a t_belief_header bit, see event.h) - which pins it across the normal
; salience decay AND retroactively flags its supporters as core episodes.
;
; .ms1 = System 1 (subconscious / appraisal) layer; see
; Docs/memory/memory_system.md for the file-type convention.
;
; Syntax:
;   (identity_driver <label> [:tar <kind>] [:weight <float>])
;     label   : the belief's relation label. Required.
;     :tar    : optional kind constraint on the target (matches kind-valued
;               targets via is_a; object-targets bypass the kind filter in
;               the MVP).
;     :weight : contribution to identity_driver_weight (default 1.0). Higher
;               weight = bigger identity-signal per matched belief.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ----- Foundational kin (the inherited part of identity) -------------------
; Kinship is foundational identity for everyone. Born into a family, you carry
; that family-position belief forever. The Victorian setting weights kin
; especially heavily (compared to a modern individualist setting).
(identity_driver mother  :weight 0.7)
(identity_driver father  :weight 0.7)
(identity_driver child   :weight 0.7)
(identity_driver sibling :weight 0.5)
(identity_driver cousin  :weight 0.3)

; ----- Romantic / chosen bonds ---------------------------------------------
; Spouse and lover are identity-bearing - "I am someone who is married to X"
; is a definitional self-statement. Friend lands lower because friendships
; come and go more readily.
(identity_driver spouse :weight 0.7)
(identity_driver lover  :weight 0.5)
(identity_driver friend :weight 0.3)

; ----- Held values + loyalties ---------------------------------------------
; The explicit identity-attitudes from States.mon (attitude). Holding the
; attitude says "I am someone who values X" - the load-bearing identity
; signal for moral / cultural alignment.
(identity_driver identity :weight 1.0)   ; the @self identity-attitude itself
(identity_driver value    :weight 0.8)   ; held virtues
; figures one looks up to - the positive-esteem stance verbs
; (admire / revere).
(identity_driver admire :weight 0.4)
(identity_driver revere :weight 0.4)

; ----- Occupation / role ---------------------------------------------------
; "I am a doctor" / "I am a docker" is a primary identity statement in the
; Victorian milieu. Job ties to the social-class framing, the obligations
; (work, dues, employer-bonds), and the home base.
(identity_driver job              :weight 0.6)
(identity_driver social_class     :weight 0.6)
(identity_driver nationality      :weight 0.5)

; ----- Origin + dwelling ---------------------------------------------------
; Where you were born and where you live are identity markers in this
; setting - the parish, the home, the family seat all anchor self-image.
(identity_driver birthplace :weight 0.4)
(identity_driver home       :weight 0.4)

; ----- Long-burn pressures (residual identity load) ------------------------
; A standing humiliation pressure, an enduring grief, a chronic injustice
; sense - these become part of who someone IS over time. Phase 9 will use
; the accumulated weight here to anchor the long-burn pressure beliefs that
; appraisal mints when bad things keep happening.
(identity_driver pressure :weight 0.3)
